  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Monday through Wednesday trends drier with warming in the\n  mountains. Meanwhile, low clouds and fog will likely linger for\n  valleys.\n\n- Next system potentially impacting travel over passes on\n  Thanksgiving morning.\n\nHigh pressure building over north central Idaho and western \nMontana will strengthen valley inversions through Wednesday. \nThese inversions will trap cold air, low clouds, and fog in valley\nlocations. As the high pressure reaches peak strength on Tuesday,\nlow clouds may descend into widespread fog, creating significant \nvisibility reductions. Where this fog and cloud cover persists \nthroughout the day, daytime heating and high temperatures will be \nnoticeably limited.\n\nThe next weather system, driven by a weakening atmospheric river, \nwill arrive on Thanksgiving morning. Initial snow levels around \n4,000 feet will gradually rise throughout the morning. Valley \nlocations will experience breezy conditions by the afternoon with \nmostly light precipitation due to downslope winds. Because trapped\ncold air will be the slowest to erode in northwest Montana, \nvalleys in this region may see a brief period of light midday snow\nbefore transitioning to rain. Meanwhile, the mountains will \nreceive steady precipitation through Saturday. Expect a few inches\nof accumulation at Lolo, Lookout, and Lost Trail Passes for \nThanksgiving morning. Because atmospheric river systems can \noccasionally exceed expected snowfall totals, please monitor \nforecast updates for your holiday travel planning.\n\nBy Friday, snow levels will climb to between 5,000 and 6,000 feet, \nallowing for a few additional inches of snow at higher \nelevations. Another surge of moisture is anticipated across the \nregion from Saturday into Sunday, though snow levels for this \nweekend system remain uncertain at this time.",
